  "name": null,
  "problem": "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})}=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14262363/assets/ae746b3869a36bef6740e75f1bd6c82ebf296d3a65de5860cd9dc43fc09f3c38.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the right, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}={lim}_{x\\to \\,{\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=-\\infty .\n$$",
